1
00:00:00,211 --> 00:00:01,044
Two crashes.

2
00:00:01,597 --> 00:00:02,129
One death.

3
00:00:02,481 --> 00:00:04,138
And Senna still got in the car.

4
00:00:06,291 --> 00:00:10,518
This is his story, from a go kart in
São Paulo to a courtroom in Rome.

5
00:00:18,831 --> 00:00:21,390
Ayrton Senna was a
Brazilian racing driver

6
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:24,159
and a three time Formula
One world champion.

7
00:00:26,351 --> 00:00:30,188
He was born in São
Paulo on 21 March 1960,

8
00:00:30,559 --> 00:00:32,503
and began racing karts at thirteen,

9
00:00:32,523 --> 00:00:35,959
in a kart his father built
around a lawnmower engine.

10
00:00:38,131 --> 00:00:41,691
He won the South American
Kart Championship in 1977,

11
00:00:42,002 --> 00:00:44,519
and twice finished runner up
at the world championship.

12
00:00:46,811 --> 00:00:50,067
In 1981 he moved to England
for single seater racing,

13
00:00:50,378 --> 00:00:53,495
and in 1983 he won the
British Formula Three title,

14
00:00:53,685 --> 00:00:55,599
after a close fight with Martin Brundle.

15
00:00:57,891 --> 00:01:02,719
He made his Formula One debut in
1984, with the small Toleman team.

16
00:01:04,891 --> 00:01:07,085
At Monaco that year, in heavy rain,

17
00:01:07,476 --> 00:01:10,002
he started thirteenth
and finished second,

18
00:01:10,022 --> 00:01:13,499
closing fast on the leader Alain
Prost when the race was stopped.

19
00:01:15,871 --> 00:01:19,912
In April 1985, driving for Lotus,
he won his first Grand Prix,

20
00:01:20,003 --> 00:01:21,938
in the wet at Estoril, in Portugal.

21
00:01:24,371 --> 00:01:27,804
In 1986, on a Brazilian
television programme,

22
00:01:27,824 --> 00:01:29,298
he explained why he raced.

23
00:01:39,811 --> 00:01:43,548
In 1988 he joined McLaren,
alongside Alain Prost.

24
00:01:44,119 --> 00:01:47,215
The two won fifteen of the
sixteen races that season,

25
00:01:47,326 --> 00:01:49,279
and Senna took his first world title.

26
00:01:51,351 --> 00:01:54,879
He won at Monaco six times,
more than any other driver.

27
00:01:56,810 --> 00:01:58,784
His rivalry with Prost was fierce.

28
00:01:59,396 --> 00:02:03,033
At Suzuka in 1989, and again in 1990,

29
00:02:03,243 --> 00:02:06,419
the two collided, and each time
it settled the championship.

30
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:13,288
He won the world title
in 1988, 1990 and 1991.

31
00:02:13,839 --> 00:02:17,435
By 1994 he had won forty
one Grand Prix races

32
00:02:17,506 --> 00:02:19,799
and taken sixty five pole positions.

33
00:02:22,051 --> 00:02:24,677
In the rain at Donington in 1993,

34
00:02:24,697 --> 00:02:29,399
he passed four drivers on the first lap
to take the lead, and went on to win.

35
00:02:31,871 --> 00:02:36,655
On 28 March 1993, he won the
Brazilian Grand Prix at Interlagos,

36
00:02:36,826 --> 00:02:38,059
in front of his home crowd.

37
00:02:40,051 --> 00:02:44,139
When the race ended, Brazilian
fans ran onto the track to celebrate.

38
00:02:46,091 --> 00:02:49,919
For 1994, he left McLaren
for a new team, Williams.

39
00:02:52,031 --> 00:02:53,724
But the season started badly.

40
00:02:54,135 --> 00:02:57,211
He took pole position in
both of the first two races,

41
00:02:57,542 --> 00:03:01,239
and finished neither, so after
two races he had no points.

42
00:03:06,652 --> 00:03:09,539
The weekend began
on Friday, 29 April.

43
00:03:11,471 --> 00:03:14,700
The young Brazilian driver
Rubens Barrichello lost control

44
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:17,458
of his Jordan on a kerb
and hit a safety fence.

45
00:03:19,831 --> 00:03:22,247
He survived, with a
broken nose and arm,

46
00:03:22,458 --> 00:03:26,299
after the circuit's chief doctor, Sid
Watkins, reached him quickly.

47
00:03:28,473 --> 00:03:30,738
Senna went straight to
check on his compatriot.

48
00:03:32,851 --> 00:03:36,978
Then Senna went back to his car, and
set the fastest time in qualifying.

49
00:03:39,291 --> 00:03:41,425
On Saturday, 30 April,

50
00:03:41,575 --> 00:03:45,042
the Austrian driver Roland
Ratzenberger crashed in qualifying,

51
00:03:45,062 --> 00:03:45,974
and died.

52
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:48,679
He drove for the small team Simtek.

53
00:03:50,791 --> 00:03:54,710
Qualifying was stopped for almost
fifty minutes, and when it resumed,

54
00:03:54,801 --> 00:03:58,199
several teams, including Senna's
Williams, took no further part.

55
00:04:00,551 --> 00:04:04,899
It was the first death at a Formula
One race weekend since 1982.

56
00:04:07,091 --> 00:04:10,699
Watkins later wrote that he urged
Senna to give up racing altogether.

57
00:04:12,871 --> 00:04:14,617
Watkins wrote that Senna answered

58
00:04:14,637 --> 00:04:16,304
that some things are
out of our control,

59
00:04:16,324 --> 00:04:17,999
and that he could not quit.

60
00:04:20,171 --> 00:04:23,667
On Sunday morning, he filmed an
in car lap for French television,

61
00:04:24,098 --> 00:04:26,612
where his old rival Alain
Prost was working.

62
00:04:27,184 --> 00:04:30,459
He greeted Prost on camera,
and Prost said he was surprised.

63
00:04:32,692 --> 00:04:35,417
Senna started Sunday's
race from pole position.

64
00:04:37,771 --> 00:04:43,118
On Sunday, 1 May 1994, an accident at
the start brought out the safety car.

65
00:04:45,512 --> 00:04:48,498
Racing resumed on lap
six, with Senna leading.

66
00:04:50,711 --> 00:04:54,114
On lap seven, his Williams left the
track at the Tamburello corner,

67
00:04:54,285 --> 00:04:55,339
and the race was stopped.

68
00:04:57,451 --> 00:05:00,338
We show it once, from a
distance, and no more.

69
00:05:02,572 --> 00:05:04,010
Ayrton Senna died that day.

70
00:05:04,101 --> 00:05:05,137
He was thirty four.

71
00:05:07,391 --> 00:05:11,098
Sources disagree on the exact time
of death, so we do not give one.

72
00:05:13,351 --> 00:05:16,739
The race was restarted, and
Michael Schumacher won it.

73
00:05:21,851 --> 00:05:27,079
His body was flown home, and on 5
May, São Paulo held his state funeral.

74
00:05:29,371 --> 00:05:31,627
Brazil declared days
of national mourning,

75
00:05:31,858 --> 00:05:34,374
and the coffin crossed
the city on a fire engine,

76
00:05:34,525 --> 00:05:36,079
draped in the Brazilian flag.

77
00:05:38,291 --> 00:05:41,628
More than two hundred thousand
people visited him as he lay in state,

78
00:05:42,079 --> 00:05:44,304
and estimates of the
crowd at the funeral run

79
00:05:44,324 --> 00:05:46,639
from one million to
about three million.

80
00:05:48,791 --> 00:05:52,051
Rivals carried his coffin,
among them Alain Prost,

81
00:05:52,141 --> 00:05:54,579
Gerhard Berger and
Rubens Barrichello.

82
00:05:56,811 --> 00:05:59,770
The Tamburello corner
was rebuilt as a chicane,

83
00:05:59,921 --> 00:06:02,319
and the drivers safety
group was re-established.

84
00:06:04,490 --> 00:06:09,859
In November 1994, his family
founded the Instituto Ayrton Senna,

85
00:06:09,879 --> 00:06:12,003
led by his sister Viviane.

86
00:06:12,023 --> 00:06:16,159
It works on education for children and
teenagers from low income families.

87
00:06:18,471 --> 00:06:23,783
Italian prosecutors investigated,
and a judicial report in 1995 pointed

88
00:06:23,803 --> 00:06:26,679
at a steering column failure
after a pre-race adjustment.

89
00:06:28,870 --> 00:06:32,185
A trial opened at Imola
in February 1997.

90
00:06:32,616 --> 00:06:34,469
Six men were charged
with manslaughter:

91
00:06:34,579 --> 00:06:36,432
the Williams team
owner Frank Williams,

92
00:06:36,562 --> 00:06:38,155
technical director Patrick Head,

93
00:06:38,305 --> 00:06:40,999
designer Adrian Newey,
and three race organisers.

94
00:06:43,391 --> 00:06:45,088
The case centred on
the steering column,

95
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:47,438
which had been lengthened
at Senna's request.

96
00:06:49,731 --> 00:06:53,759
On 16 December 1997,
all six were acquitted.

97
00:06:55,831 --> 00:06:57,685
In January 2003,

98
00:06:58,116 --> 00:07:01,564
Italy's Supreme Court of Cassation
overturned the acquittals

99
00:07:01,584 --> 00:07:03,659
of Head and Newey and
ordered a new appeal.

100
00:07:06,091 --> 00:07:10,879
In May 2005, the new appeal
court found Newey not guilty.

101
00:07:12,991 --> 00:07:15,327
Head appealed again,
to be fully cleared.

102
00:07:16,019 --> 00:07:19,699
In April 2007, the Supreme
Court rejected that appeal.

103
00:07:22,050 --> 00:07:22,491
Reports

104
00:07:22,511 --> 00:07:26,767
of the ruling say the court found the
steering modification poorly designed

105
00:07:26,817 --> 00:07:28,149
and poorly executed,

106
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:31,093
and that a steering column
failure caused the accident.

107
00:07:31,644 --> 00:07:35,579
It held Head responsible for a failure
of control, but too late to punish.

108
00:07:37,771 --> 00:07:41,180
Williams has always argued that
the column broke only on impact.

109
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:44,499
Head himself believed that
Senna had made a driving error.

110
00:07:46,811 --> 00:07:50,052
So after nearly thirteen years,
the courts named a cause,

111
00:07:50,263 --> 00:07:51,879
and the team still disputes it.

112
00:07:55,091 --> 00:07:57,259
The highest court said a
steering column failed,

113
00:07:57,279 --> 00:08:00,019
but the team says it
broke only on impact.

114
00:08:01,191 --> 00:08:03,268
After nearly thirteen years of trials,

115
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:06,199
who gets to say what really
happened at Tamburello?

116
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:36,460
In the end, it is a pleasure: something good that brings positive energy. (our translation)
